# Hire-Me Agent Rules

Before changing code:

1. Read `AGENTS.md`.
2. Read `PROJECT_MEMORY.md`.
3. Read:
   - `docs/project/STATUS.md`
   - `docs/project/HANDOFF.md`
   - `docs/project/DECISIONS.md`
   - `docs/project/RISKS.md`
   - `docs/project/ROADMAP.md`
4. Read the GitHub issue being implemented completely.
5. Inspect current `main` and relevant merged implementation before making assumptions.
6. Use repository skills referenced by `AGENTS.md` when applicable.

## Git discipline

- One GitHub issue per feature branch.
- Start from the latest `origin/main`.
- Never develop directly on `main`.
- Never merge a PR automatically.
- Never deploy.
- Never force-push shared history unless explicitly approved.
- Never discard unrelated user/developer changes.
- Keep the PR open for human/ChatGPT review.
- Prefer a draft PR for implementation work.

## Architecture

- Prisma belongs only to the API workspace.
- Shared contracts and the web app must remain Prisma-independent.
- Reuse existing modules, authorization policies, storage abstractions, audit infrastructure, and domain models.
- Do not introduce a second source of truth.
- Do not create parallel models for concepts already represented in the repository.
- Business records and managed `Document` files are separate concepts unless the product explicitly links them.

## Database

- Additive migrations only.
- Never modify a migration already merged to `main`.
- Use PostgreSQL constraints/transactions/locking for important integrity and concurrency rules.
- Do not rely only on application-level checks when database integrity can enforce the invariant.
- Test concurrency-sensitive behavior against real PostgreSQL.

## Security

Treat these as merge blockers:

- IDOR / record-scope bypasses
- permission bypasses
- aggregate/count side-channel leakage
- sensitive data exposure
- cross-client or cross-context linkage
- unsafe file/storage access
- non-atomic sensitive mutations
- stale authorization races
- insecure exports

Authorization must be server-authoritative.

UI permission gating is never sufficient by itself.

Prefer failing closed when authorization is ambiguous.

## Product boundaries

Do not silently expand issue scope.

Preserve existing product decisions and merged behavior.

Do not implement future modules merely because the schema contains placeholders.

When product behavior is genuinely ambiguous:
- stop that specific decision;
- document the question;
- do not invent business rules.

## Tests

Every meaningful security/business rule should have regression coverage.

For database work, cover:
- happy path
- unauthorized path
- cross-record mismatch / IDOR
- archived/stale context
- rejected writes leaving no side effects
- concurrency/retry behavior where relevant

Do not delete or weaken existing tests just to make CI pass.

## Validation

Before declaring completion, run the repository-required checks, including applicable equivalents of:

- format check
- `git diff --check`
- Prisma validate
- Prisma generate
- architecture boundary check
- lint
- typecheck
- unit tests
- build
- PostgreSQL migrations from clean state
- seed twice
- PostgreSQL integration tests
- Mermaid validation for changed Mermaid diagrams

If something cannot run locally, say exactly why and rely on GitHub Actions for that portion.

## Pull request

The PR body must contain:

- `Closes #<issue>`
- implementation summary
- security/authorization design
- schema/migration changes, if any
- test coverage
- local validation
- exact-head GitHub Actions evidence when available
- known limitations / explicitly out-of-scope work

Never claim checks passed unless they actually ran successfully.

## Completion report

Report:

- issue
- branch
- base `main` SHA
- commits
- PR number/link
- final head SHA
- files changed
- architecture/design decisions
- security decisions
- migrations, if any
- tests added
- PostgreSQL test total
- validation results
- CI run/jobs
- blockers/questions
- confirmation PR is open and unmerged

End with:

`Ready for ChatGPT review: YES/NO`
